'''Jorá''' (Hora) is an extinct [[Tupi–Guaraní languages|Tupi–Guaraní language]] of [[Bolivia]]. It is closely related to [[Sirionó language|Sirionó]] and [[Yuqui language|Yuqui]]. Its speakers were exterminated in a genocide perpertated against them in the mid-20th century.

== History ==
The Jorá people and langage were never numerically large.<ref name=":0">{{Cite journal |last=Danielsen |first=Swintha |last2=Gasparini |first2=Noé |date=August 2015 |title=News on the Jorá (Tupí-Guaraní): sociolinguistics, description, and classification |url=http://www.scielo.br/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S1981-81222015000200441&lng=en&tlng=en |journal=Boletim do Museu Paraense Emílio Goeldi. Ciências Humanas |volume=10 |issue=2 |pages=441–466 |doi=10.1590/1981-81222015000200012 |issn=1981-8122}}</ref> In the 1940s, the Austrian anthropologist {{Interlanguage link|Wanda Hanke|de}} found out that the Jorá were being "hunted like animals" in a "systematic [[genocide]]". Previously, the Jorá lived around a lake near the town of [[Baures]], inhabited by the [[Baure people]], who feared their attacks. The Baure say they felt threatened by the Jorá and were therefore justified in their killings. The remaining Jorá were adopted by local families or sold into slavery. The remaining descendants of the Jorá do not bring up their memories, even if asked. In one case, a Jorá woman who had been found orphaned as a child had been repeatedly asked to elicit some vocabulary of the Jorá language.<ref name=":1">{{Cite conference|last=Danielsen |first=Swintha |last2=Gasparini |first2=Noé |date=2015-06-11 |title=Discovering two almost extinct languages in Bolivia: Jorá and Guarasu |url=https://univ-sorbonne-nouvelle.hal.science/hal-01495156v1 |language=en|conference=18e Rencontres Jeunes Chercheurs en Sciences du Langage, 2015-06-03|location=Paris}}</ref>

=== Documentation ===
The Jorá language is known solely from various vocabularies, mainly collected by non-linguists; the first was collected by Hanke and published in 1959, another was collected by François-Xavier Béghin in 1951 and published in 1980, and secondary data was collected in 2009 and 2011 from Baure people who had lived with the Jorá in the 1940s and 1950s.<ref name=":1" />

== Classification ==
Jorá may be classified as a [[Tupi–Guarani languages|Tupi–Guarani language]] belonging to a subgroup with [[Sirionó language|Sirionó]] and [[Yuqui language|Yuqui]]. It has, in some classifications, been grouped as a dialect of Sirionó.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":0" />

== Phonology ==

=== Vowels ===
Despite the fact that it is unattested in the corpus, the vowel {{IPA|[ɨ]}} was reported to have been much used by the Jorá, according to the Baure who remembered characteristics of Jorá. This vowel has been completely lost from [[Proto-Tupi-Guarani]] in Yuki and swapped with {{IPA|/i/}} in Sirionó. It is thus assumed that {{IPA|[ɨ]}} was simply not distinguished by the collectors of the data.<ref name=":0" />

